findings.description:
Entry: docreader/main.py:160 (ReadFromFile -> Parser.parse_file -> BaseParser.parse -> TextSplitter.split_text).
Attacker: a WeKnora user who uploads a document (or supplies a URL), crossing the boundary from user-supplied content into unbounded server-side CPU work.
Path: main.py ReadFromFile decodes request.file_content into a parser; for markdown/txt the parser returns Document(content=text) with chunks empty (Document.chunks defaults to []), so BaseParser.parse (docreader/parser/base_parser.py:345-350) builds a TextSplitter and calls split_text(document.content); split_text calls _split_protected (docreader/splitter/splitter.py:290-324), which runs pattern.finditer(text) for every entry in the default protected_regex list over the entire attacker text.
Why nothing stops it: the default patterns use nested quantifiers over a single line, e.g. r"(?:\|[^|
]*)+\|[
]+" and r"(?:\|[^|
]*)+\|[
]+\s*(?:\|\s*:?-{3,}:?\s*)+\|[
]+". A markdown line that is a long run of '|' characters with no trailing newline makes the engine try exponentially many ways to partition the pipes between the inner group and the trailing '\|', causing catastrophic backtracking. No input validation, timeout, or size check runs before this, and the same text is scanned once per protected pattern.
Impact: a single small request pins a gRPC worker thread (only GRPC_MAX_WORKERS, default 4, exist) for an unbounded time, so a handful of such uploads stalls all document parsing for the service.
Fix: replace the ambiguous nested-quantifier patterns with possessive/atomic or linear alternatives (e.g. r"\|(?:[^|
]*\|)+[
]+" with a leading anchor, or split lines and test each row), or cap the text length fed to _split_protected and run the regex with a hard time/step budget.
